<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Petrarch</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1304">1304</a>–1374</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Francesco Petrarca</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Petrarch, Francesco Petrarca, was an Italian poet and scholar born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a> in 1304. His Italian love poems to Laura, collected in the Canzoniere, shaped European lyric poetry for centuries, and his search for classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> made him a founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> humanism. Crowned poet laureate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1341, he spent his last years near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> under the patronage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a> lords.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/petrarch.jpg" alt="Petrarch" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Francesco_Petrarca_(called_Petrarch)_-_French_school,_17th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
